<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hōjō Ujitsuna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1487">1487</a>–<a href="/years/1541">1541</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hojo Ujitsuna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hōjō Ujitsuna was the second head of the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hojo clan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, son of Hojo Soun. He adopted the surname Hojo, recalling the regents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, and expanded the family's lands from Sagami into Musashi, taking Edo castle in 1524. He strengthened the castle town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and built up an efficient administration that his son Ujiyasu developed further.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hojo-ujitsuna.jpg" alt="Hōjō Ujitsuna" width="320" height="405" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ujituna_Hojo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ujitsuna was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-soun">Hōjō Sōun</a>, the founder of the Go-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, and he carried on his father's effort to win control of the Kantō, the region around present-day Tokyo. In 1524 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a> from Uesugi Tomooki, which began a long rivalry between the Hōjō and Uesugi families. In 1526 he was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-nobutora">Takeda Nobutora</a> at the Battle of Nashinokidaira.</p><p>The Uesugi then attacked and burned the shrine of Tsurugaoka Hachimangū in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>. The loss weighed heavily on the Hōjō as a symbol, because the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-clan">Hōjō clan</a>, from which they had taken their name, had fallen in the Siege of Kamakura in 1333. Ujitsuna soon began rebuilding the shrine, a work completed in 1540. In 1530 his son Ujiyasu defeated Uesugi Tomooki at the Battle of Ozawahara.</p><p>When the Uesugi attacked Edo again in 1535, while Ujitsuna was away fighting the Takeda, he returned, defeated Tomooki and recovered his lands. After Tomooki died in 1537, Ujitsuna occupied Musashi Province and seized Kawagoe Castle to secure his hold on the Kantō. In 1538 he won the Battle of Kōnodai, which secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimosa-province">Shimōsa Province</a> for the Hōjō, and in 1539 he defeated Yoshiaki, the Oyumi Kubo, and gained control of Awa Province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the years before his death on 10 August 1541, Ujitsuna oversaw the rebuilding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a>, which he made a symbol of the growing power of the Hōjō, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and Edo. He was succeeded as head of the clan and lord of Odawara by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hojo-ujiyasu">Hōjō Ujiyasu</a>, his son by his wife Yōjuin-dono. His other children included Hōjō Tamemasa, Hōjō Ujitaka and the adopted Hōjō Tsunashige, and his daughters married into other houses; one of them, Hōshun’in, became the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-haruuji">Ashikaga Haruuji</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hōjō Ujitsuna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Ujitsuna</b> adopted the prestigious surname Hojo, recalling the Kamakura regents, though the families were unrelated.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-hojo-clan-domain">Later Hojo clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Odawara Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.25,139.15&amp;year=1522" aria-label="Odawara Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hōjō Ujitsuna"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4247 4516h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4247 4516h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.25,139.15&amp;year=1522">What was here in 1522? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
